.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
.B ABS(\|)
returns the absolute value of its floating point argument
.IR f.
.LP
.B boolean_alloc(\|)
returns a pointer to a block of boolean of at least size
.IR n,
which is appropriately aligned.
.LP
.B byte_alloc(\|)
returns a pointer to a block of bytes of at least size
.IR n,
which is appropriately aligned.
.LP
.B char_alloc(\|)
returns a pointer to a block of char of at least size
.IR n,
which is appropriately aligned.
.LP
.B DEQ(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if the difference between its double precision arguments
.I f1
and
.I f2
is less than 
.SM DEPSILON.
.LP
.B DGE(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its double precision argument 
.I f1
is greater than or equal to
.I f2.
.LP
.B DGT(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its double precision argument 
.I f1
is greater than 
.I f2
by a margin of no less than
.SM EPSILON.
.LP
.B DLE(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its double precision argument 
.I f1
is less than or equal to 
.I f2.
.LP
.B DLT(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its double precision argument 
.I f1
is less than 
.I f2
by a margin of at least
.SM DEPSILON.
.LP
.B DNE(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its double precision arguments
.I f1
and
.I f2
differ by a margin of at least
.SM DEPSILON.
.LP
.B double_alloc(\|)
a pointer to a block of double precision numbers of at least size
.IR n,
which is appropriately aligned.
 .LP
.LP
.B EVEN(\|)
takes an integer argument
.I i
and returns
.SM true 
if the integer is even, or
.SM FALSE
otherwise.
.LP
.B FEQ(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if the difference between its floating point arguments
.I f1
and
.I f2
is less than 
.SM EPSILON.
.LP
.B FGE(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its floating point argument 
.I f1
is greater than or equal to
.I f2.
.LP
.B FGT(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its floating point argument 
.I f1
is greater than 
.I f2
by a margin of no less than
.SM EPSILON.
.LP
.B FLE(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its floating point argument 
.I f1
is less than or equal to 
.I f2.
.LP
.B FLT(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its floating point argument 
.I f1
is less than 
.I f2
by a margin of at least
.SM EPSILON.
.LP
.B FNE(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE
if its floating point arguments
.I f1
and
.I f2
differ by a margin of at least
.SM EPSILON.
.LP
.B float_alloc(\|)
a pointer to a block of floating point numbers of at least size
.IR n,
which is appropriately aligned.
.LP
.B gen_alloc(\|)
returns a pointer to a block of at least
.I size
bytes, which is appropriately aligned.
.LP
.B gen_calloc(\|)
allocates space  for  an  array  of
.I nelem
elements of size
.IR elsize,
initializes the space to zeros, and returns a pointer to 
the initialized block.  The block can be freed with 
.B gen_free(\|).
.LP
.B gen_free(\|)
releases a previously allocated block of memory.  Its argument
.I ptr
is a pointer to a block previously allocated by
.B boolean_alloc(\|), byte_alloc(\|), char_alloc(\|), double_alloc(\|),
.B float_alloc(\|), gen_alloc(\|), gen_calloc(\|), gen_realloc(\|),
.B int_alloc(\|), ptr_alloc(\|), 
and
.B short_alloc(\|).
.LP
.B gen_realloc(\|)
changes the size of the block referenced by 
.I ptr
to
.I size
bytes and returns a pointer to the (possibly moved) block.
The contents will be unchanged up to the lesser of the new
and old sizes.  If unable to honor a reallocation request, 
.B gen_realloc(\|) 
leaves its first argument unaltered.  For backwards compatibility,
.B gen_realloc(\|)
accepts a pointer to a block freed since the most recent call to
.B boolean_alloc(\|), byte_alloc(\|), char_alloc(\|), double_alloc(\|),
.B float_alloc(\|), gen_alloc(\|), gen_calloc(\|), gen_realloc(\|),
.B int_alloc(\|), ptr_alloc(\|),
and
.B short_alloc(\|).
Note: using 
.B gen_realloc(\|) with a block freed before the most recent call to
.B boolean_alloc(\|), byte_alloc(\|), char_alloc(\|), double_alloc(\|),
.B float_alloc(\|), gen_alloc(\|), gen_calloc(\|), gen_realloc(\|),
.B int_alloc(\|), ptr_alloc(\|),
or
.B short_alloc(\|),
will result in an error.
.LP
.B gen_zero(\|)
sets the first 
.I i 
characters at memory location
.I ptr
ot the value 0.  It returns
.IR ptr.
.LP
.B int_alloc(\|)
a pointer to a block of int of at least size
.IR n,
which is appropriately aligned.
.LP
.B MAX(\|)
returns the greater of its two arguments
.I i1
and
.IR i2.
.LP
.B MIN(\|)
returns the lesser of its two arguments
.I i1
and
.IR i2
.LP
.B ptr_alloc(\|)
a pointer to a block of pointers of at least size
.IR n,
which is appropriately aligned.
.LP
.B short_alloc(\|)
a pointer to a block of short integers of at least size
.IR n,
which is appropriately aligned.
.LP
.B ROUND(\|)
adds 0.5 to its floating point argument
.I f
and truncates the result, returning an integer.	
.LP
.B SIGN(\|)
evaluates the sign of its floating point argument
.I f
and returns an integer; -1, if
.I f
is negative, and 1 if
.I f is positive.
.LP
.B SWAP(\|)
exchanges the values of its floating point arguments
.I f1
and
.IR f2.
.LP
.B XOR(\|)
performs an exclusive-or operation on its boolean arguments
.I b1
and
.IR b2,
returning 
.SM TRUE
if one and only one of its arguments is 
.SM TRUE.

.SH RETURN VALUES
.LP
On success, 
.B boolean_alloc(\|), byte_alloc(\|), char_alloc(\|), double_alloc(\|),
.B float_alloc(\|), gen_alloc(\|), gen_calloc(\|), gen_realloc(\|),
.B int_alloc(\|), ptr_alloc(\|),
and
.B short_alloc(\|),
return a pointer to space suitably aligned for storage for their given 
type of object.  On failure, they return NULL.
			 
.SH NOTES
.LP
.SM EPSILON
is defined with the value 0.00001 or 1.0E-5.
.LP
.SM DEPSILON
is defined with the value 0.00000000000001 or 1.0E-14.

